#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""fleet-plan-review — a plan-filing PR's review survives the session that
filed it (plan 0188).

A plan is filed as a pull request that is itself nothing but prose: the new
plan document, maybe an ADR, maybe a line in another plan's `depends_on`.
Today that review happens only if the filing session is still alive when CI
goes green, or a human remembers to run `dispatch-review.py` by hand. Neither
survives the session ending, which it always does — CI takes minutes, the
session does not wait.

This is the durable queue and its bounded worker:

  fleet-plan-review enqueue <owner/repo> <pr> [--db PATH]
  fleet-plan-review status [--repo R] [--pr N] [--json] [--db PATH]
  fleet-plan-review worker [--limit N] [--claim-ttl SECS] [--db PATH]

`enqueue` is idempotent on (repo, pr, head_sha) — filing twice, or a sweep
that finds the same open PR twice, writes one row. `worker` runs ONE bounded
pass: it claims up to `--limit` ready jobs (default 5), re-reads each one's
ground truth from the forge, and either dispatches, holds, skips, or finishes
it. It is not a daemon and this file ships no launchd unit — see "Automatic
dispatch" below for why.

## What a job holds for, skips, or dispatches

Eligibility is re-derived from the forge on every claim, never trusted from
what `enqueue` saw:

  - closed, merged or draft            -> skipped, terminal
  - repo not in config/repos.conf      -> skipped, terminal
  - the changed-file list is incomplete or unreadable
                                        -> held, re-read next sweep
  - the change is not plan-only (talos's `reviewer_position` would hand it to
    `code_reviewer`, not `plan_reviewer`)
                                        -> skipped, terminal — it is not a
                                           plan filing, so nothing here is
                                           the right reviewer for it
  - CI pending, red, or unreadable      -> held, re-read next sweep (a red
                                           build is rechecked, not abandoned:
                                           the fix for it is usually a second
                                           push to the SAME head's status, and
                                           a job here is keyed by head only
                                           when the push actually moves it).
                                           `eunomia/deps` (plan 0107) is
                                           dropped from this check before it
                                           runs: ADR-0028 §4 says a plan PR's
                                           dependency edges gate DISPATCH onto
                                           main, not review, so a filing whose
                                           named plan or `depends_on` is not
                                           yet met still gets its review.
  - the head moved since this row was filed
                                        -> superseded, terminal for this row;
                                           a fresh row is enqueued for the new
                                           head
  - a live review from EVERY currently configured plan-reviewer seat
    (`bin/fleet-models`' `repo_model` for `plan_reviewer`, ordinal 1 the
    `reviewer` role's account, ordinal 2 `second_reviewer`'s — ADR-0028 §1:
    only each ordinal's own review settles that ordinal) already stands at
    this head (talos's own `post-review.py` idempotence notwithstanding — a
    crash between posting and recording completion must not pay for a second
    round)
                                        -> done, no spend
  - CI green (or the repo configures none at all) and nothing above fired
                                        -> dispatched through the SAME
                                           `dispatch_review()` the orchestrator
                                           uses — which runs every configured
                                           seat in one round, skipping
                                           whichever ordinal already has a
                                           standing review (talos's own
                                           `standing_ordinals()`), so a prior
                                           partial round is completed, not
                                           re-paid for

## Automatic dispatch — why this ships with no launchd unit

Plan 0188 D4 requires proving coordination with mopsus's retry lock and
Talos's standing review checks, and says plainly: if the existing contracts
cannot prevent two live launches, name the missing one and stop before
wiring automatic dispatch. That check was made, not assumed:

  - mopsus's one-at-a-time slot (`bin/mopsus`'s `_review_slot`, ADR-0016 §4)
    is taken here before every dispatch (see `_dispatch_one`), and the
    pending-ordinal check is RE-READ immediately after taking it, so a
    caller that ALSO takes this same slot before posting (mopsus's own
    `_dispatch_session_retry`, its `kind="session"` retry) can never post
    between this worker's decision to spend and the spend itself.
  - `dispatch-review.py` posts through `post-review.py`'s own per-SHA
    idempotence, and this worker re-reads `reviews_at()` immediately before
    taking the slot, again right after taking it, and again after spending,
    so a review already standing at the head is read, never re-paid for.
  - `dispatch-review.py` itself DOES check for an existing review before
    posting — `standing_ordinals()` (read at ~/agent 2db65f5, line 4391; read
    from `/repos/{repo}/pulls/{pr}/reviews` at line 4405; used in `main()` at
    lines 5121-5162, which skips any ordinal already approved at the head
    and `sys.exit(0)`s before posting anything if every configured ordinal's
    own approval already stands, line 5161-5162). That is a real check, not
    none — "grepped: no `list_reviews` call" is true of the literal name but
    false of the behaviour; `standing_ordinals()` IS the pre-post check,
    reading the SAME `/pulls/{pr}/reviews` endpoint `list_reviews` elsewhere
    in this fleet wraps.
  - But that check is a single read with no lock around it: two
    `dispatch-review.py` processes started close enough together can each
    read "nothing stands yet" before either has posted, and both then spend.
    THAT — a TOCTOU in `standing_ordinals()`, not an absent check — is the
    real gap, and it is what a hand-run `dispatch-review.py` on the same
    (repo, pr, head), the orchestrator's own first-attempt review loop
    (`orchestrator.py`'s `dispatch_review`, called with no lock at all for a
    PR it owns), AND a resumed orchestrator session reached through mopsus's
    OWN `kind="plan"` retry (see below) can all fall into — none of those
    three takes mopsus's slot or any other lock before reaching it.
  - The `kind="plan"` case is not hypothetical for this worker's own jobs: a
    plan-filing PR is routinely itself the deliverable of a dispatched
    implementer session (this very plan's filing is), so it CAN carry an
    orchestrator lease. `bin/mopsus`'s `_resume_review_path` takes the slot
    only to decide whether to retry and to write a `resume_request` row
    (`_write_resume_request`) — it never dispatches inside that slot for
    this kind. The actual spend happens LATER, in a brand-new
    `orchestrator --resume` process `fleet-watch`'s `orphan_sweep` starts
    after consuming that row, and THAT process reaches its own unlocked
    first-attempt review loop — the exact same uncovered path named above
    for an orchestrator-owned PR, just reached one step later. So "mopsus's
    slot" only ever serializes this worker against mopsus's `kind="session"`
    retry (`_dispatch_session_retry`, called from inside the SAME slot); it
    does not, and cannot, serialize this worker against a `kind="plan"`
    retry's eventual spend.

  For a genuine plan-filing PR the hand-run race is the SAME one
  `docs/plans-need-parsing/0026-reviewer-trigger.md` already names as a
  documented convention rather than a code contract ("a session about to
  dispatch by hand checks the queue first"); the orchestrator-lease race
  (direct, or via a mopsus plan-case resume) is the same missing contract
  under a different trigger. So this worker is built, tested, and safe to
  run ON DEMAND — but nothing here schedules it unattended, and no launchd
  plist is added by this plan. Wiring a timer is a later change, after that
  convention becomes a contract or the race is otherwise closed, and it is
  named here for whoever makes that change.
"""
import argparse
import importlib.machinery
import importlib.util
import json
import os
import sqlite3
import sys
import time
import uuid
from pathlib import Path

BIN = Path(__file__).resolve().parent
ACTOR = "fleet-plan-review"


def _load(name, path):
    ldr = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader(name, str(path))
    mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(importlib.util.spec_from_loader(name, ldr))
    ldr.exec_module(mod)
    return mod


lib = _load("fleetlib", BIN / "fleetlib.py")
fleetforge = _load("fleetforge", BIN / "fleetforge.py")
# `read_conf()` is fleet-repo's own parse of the one enrolment registry
# (plan 0013) — reused so "enrolled" here can never disagree with
# `fleet-repo list`. Lightweight (no sandbox or config dependency beyond
# `config/repos.conf` itself), so loaded eagerly like `lib`/`fleetforge`.
fleet_repo = _load("fleet_repo", BIN / "fleet-repo")

_ORCH = None
_MOPSUS = None
_FLEET_MODELS = None


def _orch_mod():
    """`bin/orchestrator`, loaded lazily. Importing it runs its own
    module-level `RUNNERS` table, which reads `config/implementer-sandbox.json`
    relative to `BIN` (`sandbox_settings()`, called while building `RUNNERS`)
    and raises if that file is absent — true of a bare `bin/` with no sibling
    `config/`, which `fleet-db-migrate`'s own tests build to check a NEW
    owner's table is picked up with no other change. Loading it only when a
    caller actually needs `dispatch_review()`/`reviews_at()`/`_forge()` keeps
    `SCHEMA` (all `fleet-db-migrate` needs) importable with no fleet
    environment at all, exactly like every other owner's module."""
    global _ORCH
    if _ORCH is None:
        _ORCH = _load("orchestrator", BIN / "orchestrator")
    return _ORCH


def _mopsus_mod():
    """`bin/mopsus`, loaded lazily for the same reason as `_orch_mod()` — it
    loads `orchestrator`, `fleet-watch`, `fleet-config` and `fleet-quota`
    itself. `_review_slot` is mopsus's one-at-a-time slot (ADR-0016 §4): a
    fleet-wide, non-blocking fcntl lock at the exact path mopsus itself uses
    (`lib.fleet_dir()/"locks"/"review-retry.lock"`). Loaded, not re-locked by
    hand, so a change to that path or that lock's semantics cannot drift
    between the two callers."""
    global _MOPSUS
    if _MOPSUS is None:
        _MOPSUS = _load("mopsus", BIN / "mopsus")
    return _MOPSUS


def _fleet_models_mod():
    """`bin/fleet-models`, loaded lazily — not for the same reason as
    `_orch_mod()`/`_mopsus_mod()` (it has no sandbox or RUNNERS-table startup
    cost: its own module-level code is `fleetlib` plus a handful of regexes
    and constants), but to match the pattern every other owner module here
    is loaded by, and because the only thing this worker ever calls on it
    (`active_ordinals`) is a plain SQL read it has no other reason to
    import for."""
    global _FLEET_MODELS
    if _FLEET_MODELS is None:
        _FLEET_MODELS = _load("fleet_models", BIN / "fleet-models")
    return _FLEET_MODELS

DB = os.path.expanduser(os.environ.get("FLEET_DB", str(lib.fleet_dir() / "fleet.db")))

# Additive DDL only, applied by `fleet-db-migrate` like every other owner's
# (plan 0096). `plan_review_job` holds CURRENT state, one row per (repo, pr,
# head_sha); `plan_review_job_change` is its append-only history, in the exact
# shape plan 0180's `dependency_attestation`/`dependency_attestation_change`
# established — non-null REAL Unix `created_at`/`updated_at`, an `actor` on
# every change, stamped through the one writer below (`_transition`/
# `_enqueue`), never by hand at a call site.
SCHEMA = """
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS plan_review_job (
  id          TEXT NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY,
  repo        TEXT NOT NULL,
  pr          INTEGER NOT NULL,
  head_sha    TEXT NOT NULL,
  state       TEXT NOT NULL,   -- queued|held|claimed|done|superseded|skipped|failed
  reason      TEXT,
  attempts    INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
  claimed_by  TEXT,
  claimed_at  REAL,
  created_at  REAL NOT NULL,
  updated_at  REAL NOT NULL,
  UNIQUE(repo, pr, head_sha)
);
CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS plan_review_job_repo_pr ON plan_review_job(repo, pr);
CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS plan_review_job_state ON plan_review_job(state);

CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS plan_review_job_change (
  id          TEXT NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY,
  job_id      TEXT NOT NULL,
  action      TEXT NOT NULL,   -- enqueue|claim|hold|skip|supersede|fail|done
  detail      TEXT,
  actor       TEXT NOT NULL,
  created_at  REAL NOT NULL,
  updated_at  REAL NOT NULL
);
CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS plan_review_job_change_job ON plan_review_job_change(job_id, created_at);
"""

OPEN_STATES = ("queued", "held")
# A dispatch failure that is not quota/no-verdict/budget (mopsus's own retry
# territory, ADR-0016/0021/0029) gets this many of ITS OWN attempts before
# this worker stops trying — bounded like every other retry in this fleet
# (`retry_limit`'s own default is 2; this is a worker-local constant, not a
# `fleet-config` key, because nothing here is a human-tunable policy yet).
MAX_DISPATCH_ATTEMPTS = 3
# Jobs mopsus's own retry policy owns (ADR-0016 quota, ADR-0021 no-verdict,
# plan 0029 budget) are held, not failed: redispatching here while mopsus is
# already retrying the SAME review would pay twice for one stop.
MOPSUS_OWNED_OUTCOMES = ("quota", "no-verdict", "budget")
# `orchestrator.dispatch_review`'s OWN default `timeout` (its signature, not
# re-derived from it — a change there must not silently change this worker's
# margin below without someone noticing the drift). Passed explicitly to
# every call so the two numbers can never read as "2400" here and something
# else there.
DISPATCH_TIMEOUT = 2400
# D1: a claim this worker still legitimately holds — mid-dispatch, inside
# `dispatch_review`'s own subprocess, which can run up to `DISPATCH_TIMEOUT`
# — must never be read as abandoned and reclaimed out from under it. 600s of
# headroom over the dispatch's own ceiling covers the forge reads
# `_dispatch_one` makes before ever reaching it.
DEFAULT_CLAIM_TTL = DISPATCH_TIMEOUT + 600


def _connect(path=None):
    con = sqlite3.connect(path or DB, timeout=30)
    con.execute("PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL")
    con.executescript(SCHEMA)
    return con


def _new_id():
    return uuid.uuid4().hex


def _enqueue(con, repo, pr, head_sha, *, actor=ACTOR, reason=None):
    """Idempotent on (repo, pr, head_sha): a job already tracked for this
    exact head is returned unchanged, whatever its state — re-filing a PR
    that is already queued, held or even done must not duplicate the row or
    reset its history."""
    row = con.execute(
        "SELECT id FROM plan_review_job WHERE repo=? AND pr=? AND head_sha=?",
        (repo, pr, head_sha)).fetchone()
    if row:
        return row[0], False
    now = time.time()
    job_id = _new_id()
    con.execute(
        "INSERT INTO plan_review_job (id, repo, pr, head_sha, state, reason, "
        "attempts, claimed_by, claimed_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES "
        "(?,?,?,?,'queued',?,0,NULL,NULL,?,?)",
        (job_id, repo, pr, head_sha, reason, now, now))
    con.execute(
        "INSERT INTO plan_review_job_change (id, job_id, action, detail, actor, "
        "created_at, updated_at) VALUES (?,?,?,?,?,?,?)",
        (_new_id(), job_id, "enqueue", reason, actor, now, now))
    con.commit()
    return job_id, True


def _transition(con, job_id, state, *, actor, action, reason=None,
                 bump_attempt=False, claim=False, holder=None,
                 expect_states=None, claim_cutoff=None, expect_holder=None):
    """The one writer for `plan_review_job` and `plan_review_job_change`.
    Every state change — including a claim — goes through here, so
    `created_at`/`updated_at` are stamped exactly once and every change carries
    an actor (D2).

    `expect_states`/`claim_cutoff`, given together or alone, make the write
    CONDITIONAL on the row's state still being one of `expect_states`, or
    being `claimed` with `claimed_at` older than `claim_cutoff` (an abandoned
    claim). That conditional UPDATE is the ONLY mechanism that resolves two
    workers racing for the same job: the loser's `rowcount` is 0 and this
    returns False, which its caller treats as "someone else already has it",
    never as an error (D3's atomic claim and crash recovery).

    `expect_holder`, given alone or alongside `expect_states`, additionally
    requires `claimed_by` to still equal it. `_fenced()` is the one caller
    that sets it: every write `_dispatch_one` makes is conditional on THIS
    worker still holding the claim, so a claim reclaimed out from under a
    dispatch slower than `--claim-ttl` (D1 — `dispatch_review`'s own timeout
    can exceed a short `--claim-ttl`) cannot have its outcome overwritten by
    the worker that lost that race; the reclaiming worker's own pass
    re-derives the same ground truth and writes it instead."""
    now = time.time()
    claimed_by = holder if claim else None
    claimed_at = now if claim else None
    attempt_sql = ", attempts = attempts + 1" if bump_attempt else ""
    params = [state, reason, claimed_by, claimed_at, now]
    sql = ("UPDATE plan_review_job SET state=?, reason=?, claimed_by=?, "
           f"claimed_at=?, updated_at=?{attempt_sql} WHERE id=?")
    params.append(job_id)
    if expect_states is not None:
        placeholders = ",".join("?" for _ in expect_states)
        where = f" AND (state IN ({placeholders})"
        params.extend(expect_states)
        if claim_cutoff is not None:
            where += " OR (state='claimed' AND claimed_at < ?)"
            params.append(claim_cutoff)
        where += ")"
        sql += where
    if expect_holder is not None:
        sql += " AND claimed_by=?"
        params.append(expect_holder)
    cur = con.execute(sql, params)
    if cur.rowcount != 1:
        con.rollback()
        return False
    con.execute(
        "INSERT INTO plan_review_job_change (id, job_id, action, detail, actor, "
        "created_at, updated_at) VALUES (?,?,?,?,?,?,?)",
        (_new_id(), job_id, action, reason, actor, now, now))
    con.commit()
    return True


def _fenced(con, job, holder, state, action, reason, bump_attempt=False):
    """Every write `_dispatch_one` makes to the job it was handed, conditional
    on `holder` still owning its claim (see `_transition`'s `expect_holder`).
    A lost race is not an error here any more than it is in `_claim_jobs`: it
    means some other worker's abandoned-claim recovery already took this job
    back, and that worker's own pass will reconcile it against the forge."""
    return _transition(con, job["id"], state, actor=holder, action=action,
                       reason=reason, bump_attempt=bump_attempt,
                       expect_states=("claimed",), expect_holder=holder)


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The filing classifier, ported from talos's dispatch-review.py.
#
# Source: operator/talos `dispatch-review.py`, functions `_is_prose_path` and
# `reviewer_position` (the classifier), and `changed_files_for_position` (the
# rename-pairing rule the classifier is fed). Read from ~/agent, a pinned
# checkout of that repository's `main`, at commit 2db65f5 (2026-11-04,
# "0040-subscription-reviewers-can-inspect-the-pr-checkout" merged). Ported
# rather than imported: talos is a different repository with no installed
# package this one may depend on, and ADR-0013's reviewer-position contract
# is exactly the four-way split (plan-only / mixed / renamed / unreadable)
# `tests/test_plan_review_queue.py` proves this port agrees with, verbatim
# against the cited source's own behaviour.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

_PROSE_PREFIXES = ("plans/", "docs/")
_PROMPT_BASENAMES = ("skill.md", "agents.md")


def _is_prose_path(path):
    """Verbatim port of talos `_is_prose_path` (see module header)."""
    if not path.startswith(_PROSE_PREFIXES):
        return False
    if not path.lower().endswith(".md"):
        return False
    parts = path.split("/")
    if any(part.lower() == ".claude" for part in parts):
        return False
    basename = parts[-1].lower()
    if basename.startswith("claude") or basename in _PROMPT_BASENAMES:
        return False
    return True


def reviewer_position(paths):
    """Verbatim port of talos `reviewer_position` (see module header):
    `plan_reviewer` when `paths` is non-empty and every path in it is prose
    under `plans/` or `docs/`, else `code_reviewer`."""
    return ("plan_reviewer" if paths and all(_is_prose_path(p) for p in paths)
            else "code_reviewer")


def changed_paths_for_position(forge, repo, pr):
    """(paths, complete, reason) — the change set `reviewer_position()`
    judges, adapted from talos `changed_files_for_position` to this module's
    own `fleetforge.Forge.list_pull_files` (which already pages to a
    confirmed-empty page or fails closed to `(0, None)`, so there is no
    separate ceiling case to track here the way talos's hand-rolled pager
    needs one).

    Each renamed entry contributes BOTH its new filename and its
    `previous_filename`, so a file moved INTO or OUT OF `plans/`/`docs/` is
    judged by what it used to be too — the exact fix talos made on eunomia#509
    for a forge that reports a move as a `deleted` entry plus an unrelated
    `added` one as often as a true `renamed` entry with both names on it."""
    st, files = forge.list_pull_files(repo, pr)
    if st != 200 or not isinstance(files, list):
        return [], False, f"the changed-file list is unavailable from the forge (status {st})"
    paths, missing = [], 0
    for f in files:
        name = f.get("filename") if isinstance(f, dict) else None
        if not name:
            missing += 1
            continue
        paths.append(name)
        prev = f.get("previous_filename")
        if prev:
            paths.append(prev)
    if missing:
        return paths, False, f"{missing} changed-file entry(ies) carried no filename"
    return paths, True, None


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A minimal CI verdict, ported (simplified) from talos's `ci_verdict`.
#
# Source: same file and commit as above, function `ci_verdict`. Simplified:
# this drops talos's REQUIRED_CI context check and returns a bare state
# instead of talos's (ok, message) pair, because this caller needs to tell
# "pending" from "red" from "absent" apart (D3: both pending and red CI hold
# and are rechecked; absent CI is a go), where talos's two-value return
# collapses pending and red into one `False`.
#
# REQUIRED_CI dropped WITHOUT a re-check elsewhere, unlike the claim this
# comment used to make: read against `~/agent` at the cited commit,
# `_required_context_missing` only escalates to a refusal (`sys.exit`) when
# `--require-ci` is given (dispatch-review.py:4896-4898); otherwise it is a
# WARN that still spends the round. `orchestrator.dispatch_review()`'s argv
# (`review_command() + [repo, str(pr), "--post", "--no-followups"]`) never
# passes `--require-ci`, so the dispatcher this worker calls does not enforce
# it either — omitting it here loses nothing a live dispatch would have kept.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

# `eunomia/deps` (plan 0107, posted by `bin/fleet-deps`) names whether a plan
# filing's OWN dependency edges — the plan it names, or that plan's
# `depends_on` — are met on `main`. That gates whether `fleet-merge` will
# MERGE the filing, never whether it gets reviewed: ADR-0028 §4, "A plan PR is
# never held, because its edges gate dispatch, not review (plan 0159)", and
# `docs/deps-status.md` "The review dispatcher": "a PR held on a dependency is
# still reviewed." A plan filing whose dependency is not yet on `main` is the
# NORMAL case for a plan that depends on one still being reviewed itself, so
# treating its `failure` as a build break would hold the review this worker
# exists to run, indefinitely, on a fact review can't change. Filtered out of
# `statuses` before any pending/red decision — never read as a vote either way.
_PLAN_HOLD_IGNORED_CONTEXTS = ("eunomia/deps",)


def ci_verdict(forge, repo, sha):
    """("green"|"pending"|"red"|"absent"|"unreadable", message)."""
    st, body = forge.get_commit_status(repo, sha)
    if st != 200 or not isinstance(body, dict):
        return "unreadable", f"CI status unreadable (forge status {st})"
    statuses = [s for s in (body.get("statuses") or [])
               if (s.get("context") or "") not in _PLAN_HOLD_IGNORED_CONTEXTS]
    if not statuses:
        return "absent", f"head {sha[:8]} has no CI status"
    failed = [s.get("context") or "?" for s in statuses
              if (s.get("status") or "").lower() in ("failure", "error")]
    if failed:
        return "red", f"CI is red on {sha[:8]}: {', '.join(failed)}"
    if any((s.get("status") or "").lower() == "pending" for s in statuses):
        return "pending", f"CI is still running on {sha[:8]}"
    return "green", f"CI green on {sha[:8]} ({len(statuses)} check(s))"


def _enrolled(repo):
    repos, _errs, _kept = fleet_repo.read_conf()
    return repo in repos


def _required_ordinals(con, repo):
    """The plan-reviewer ordinals this repo currently has configured —
    `bin/fleet-models`' own `active_ordinals(con, repo, "plan_reviewer")`, read
    directly off THIS worker's `con` (the same `repo_model` table
    `fleet-models reviewers` resolves from; no subprocess, so no risk of it
    reading a DIFFERENT `fleet.db` than this worker's own `--db`).

    Plan 0188 D3: "Honor the current plan reviewer seats." In this fleet that
    is ordinarily `[1, 2]` — `fleet-models list` carries a `default` row at
    both ordinals for `plan_reviewer`, and every per-repo override seen in
    production adds, never removes, ordinal 2. A `repo_model` table that does
    not exist at all (every test fixture here; `_connect`'s own `SCHEMA` never
    creates it) or carries no row for this repo or `'default'` falls back to
    `[1]`: the one ordinal that always exists (`_role`'s own `RoleError`
    refuses `agent_roles()` to come up without a `reviewer` account) and the
    cardinality `fleet-models`' own docstring calls `plan_reviewer`'s
    default."""
    try:
        ordinals = _fleet_models_mod().active_ordinals(con, repo, "plan_reviewer")
    except sqlite3.OperationalError:
        return [1]
    return ordinals or [1]


def _account_for_ordinal(ordinal):
    """Ordinal -> lowercased account, the SAME mapping `bin/mopsus` uses
    (`held_review_queue`, `_not_superseded_review_attempts`: `second_reviewer`
    at ordinal 2, `reviewer` otherwise) — read from `lib.agent_roles()` here
    rather than restated, so the two callers can never resolve a different
    account for the same ordinal."""
    roles = lib.agent_roles()
    role = roles["second_reviewer"] if ordinal == 2 else roles["reviewer"]
    return role.account.lower()


def _pending_ordinals(reviews, ordinals):
    """The ordinals in `ordinals` with no live review of their OWN at this
    head (`reviews`, already filtered to one SHA by `reviews_at`) yet.

    ADR-0028 §1 / ADR-0021 §4: only ordinal N's own review settles ordinal N —
    the other reviewer's review settles nothing. `reviews_at`'s own docstring
    says `rev2bot` (ordinal 2's account on every repo that has one) posts
    independently of ordinal 1, so a job is not complete just because SOME
    review stands at the head; each required ordinal needs its own."""
    logins = {((r.get("user") or {}).get("login") or "").lower() for r in reviews}
    return [o for o in ordinals if _account_for_ordinal(o) not in logins]


def _latest_review_attempt_outcome(con, repo, pr, head_sha, ordinal):
    """The most recent talos `review_attempt` row's outcome for this exact
    head AND ordinal, or None if the table does not exist or holds no such
    row. Read-only: this worker never writes talos's table, only mopsus's
    existing retry path (ADR-0016/0021/0029) does.

    Filtered to one ordinal, not merely the newest row at the head: with two
    configured seats the newest row can belong to the OTHER ordinal and prove
    nothing about this one's stop — the same identity rule mopsus's own
    `_not_superseded_review_attempts` applies with its `AND ordinal IS ?`
    (ADR-0028 §1)."""
    try:
        row = con.execute(
            "SELECT outcome FROM review_attempt WHERE repo=? AND pr=? AND "
            "head_sha=? AND ordinal IS ? ORDER BY rowid DESC LIMIT 1",
            (repo, pr, head_sha, ordinal)).fetchone()
    except sqlite3.OperationalError:
        return None
    return row[0] if row else None


def _seat_outcome(reviews, ordinal):
    """The latest review from `ordinal`'s own account at this head,
    classified `approved` or `not approved (terminal...)` — plan 0188 D5:
    "Record per-seat Forgejo outcomes. A HIGH is terminal for this head."
    `_pending_ordinals` already treats a withheld verdict (posted as
    COMMENT, never APPROVED) as settling its ordinal exactly like an
    approval — a push to a NEW head is the only thing that reopens it, never
    a redispatch at this one. This only makes that per-seat result visible in
    `reason` instead of collapsing both into a bare "posted"."""
    account = _account_for_ordinal(ordinal)
    mine = [r for r in reviews
            if ((r.get("user") or {}).get("login") or "").lower() == account]
    if not mine:
        return None
    latest = max(mine, key=lambda r: r.get("id") or 0)
    return ("approved" if latest.get("state") == "APPROVED"
            else "not approved (terminal — see the review on the pull request)")


def _seat_outcomes_reason(reviews, ordinals):
    return "; ".join(f"ordinal {o}: {_seat_outcome(reviews, o) or 'no review'}"
                     for o in ordinals)


def _owned_pending_ordinal(con, repo, pr, head_sha, pending):
    """(ordinal, outcome) for the first ordinal in `pending` whose latest
    `review_attempt` at this head is mopsus-owned (quota/no-verdict/budget),
    or (None, None) if none of them are. Checked per ordinal, never by the
    single newest row across all of them — see `_latest_review_attempt_outcome`."""
    for ordinal in pending:
        outcome = _latest_review_attempt_outcome(con, repo, pr, head_sha, ordinal)
        if outcome in MOPSUS_OWNED_OUTCOMES:
            return ordinal, outcome
    return None, None


def _dispatch_one(con, job, holder):
    """Re-derive ground truth for one claimed job and act on it. Every branch
    ends in exactly one `_fenced` call (or none, when the claim itself was
    lost to a racing worker — see `_claim_jobs`). Every write is fenced to
    `holder` (D1/D3): a claim this worker lost to an abandoned-claim
    recovery, however that happened, is left for its new holder to resolve
    rather than overwritten by whatever this worker was about to conclude."""
    repo, pr, head_sha = job["repo"], job["pr"], job["head_sha"]
    forge = _forge()

    st, pr_body = forge.get_pull(repo, pr)
    if st != 200 or not isinstance(pr_body, dict):
        _fenced(con, job, holder, "held", "hold",
               f"the pull request is unreadable from the forge (status {st})")
        return

    cur_sha = ((pr_body.get("head") or {}).get("sha")) or None
    if not cur_sha:
        # Status 200 but no readable head sha at all is the SAME "unreadable"
        # case as above, not a head move: superseding here (the old code's
        # bug) would commit no replacement row (the `if cur_sha:` guard below
        # skips `_enqueue` when it's falsy) and strand the job terminal with
        # nothing left to pick the review back up. Held, re-read next sweep.
        _fenced(con, job, holder, "held", "hold",
               "the pull request's head sha is unreadable from the forge")
        return
    if cur_sha != head_sha:
        # The replacement row is committed FIRST. `_fenced` and `_enqueue`
        # each commit on their own, so a crash between the two calls must land
        # on the side that loses nothing: if the new row exists but the old
        # one never flips to `superseded`, the old row is simply re-claimed
        # later, re-reads the same moved head, and retries this same
        # supersede (`_enqueue` is idempotent on (repo, pr, head_sha), so that
        # retry writes no duplicate). The other order — superseded committed
        # first — can crash with the old row terminal and no replacement row
        # at all, which silently drops the review this worker exists to run.
        _enqueue(con, repo, pr, cur_sha, actor=ACTOR,
                 reason="filed by supersede: the PR's head moved")
        _fenced(con, job, holder, "superseded", "supersede", f"head moved to {cur_sha}")
        return

    if pr_body.get("state") != "open" or pr_body.get("merged"):
        _fenced(con, job, holder, "skipped", "skip", f"PR is {pr_body.get('state')!r}, not open")
        return
    if pr_body.get("draft"):
        _fenced(con, job, holder, "skipped", "skip", "PR is a draft")
        return
    if not _enrolled(repo):
        _fenced(con, job, holder, "skipped", "skip",
               f"{repo} is not enrolled in config/repos.conf")
        return

    paths, complete, reason = changed_paths_for_position(forge, repo, pr)
    if not complete:
        _fenced(con, job, holder, "held", "hold", f"changed-file list: {reason}")
        return
    position = reviewer_position(paths)
    if position != "plan_reviewer":
        _fenced(con, job, holder, "skipped", "skip",
               "not a plan filing — the change touches more than "
               "plans/ or docs/ prose (talos would hand it to code_reviewer)")
        return

    ci_state, ci_message = ci_verdict(forge, repo, cur_sha)
    if ci_state in ("pending", "red", "unreadable"):
        _fenced(con, job, holder, "held", "hold", ci_message)
        return

    required = _required_ordinals(con, repo)
    reviews = _orch_mod().reviews_at(repo, pr, cur_sha, forge, fleetforge)
    if reviews is None:
        _fenced(con, job, holder, "held", "hold",
               "existing reviews at this head are unreadable from the forge")
        return
    pending = _pending_ordinals(reviews, required)
    if not pending:
        _fenced(con, job, holder, "done", "done",
               "every configured plan-reviewer seat's own review already "
               f"stands at this head ({_seat_outcomes_reason(reviews, required)})")
        return

    owned_ordinal, owned_outcome = _owned_pending_ordinal(con, repo, pr, cur_sha, pending)
    if owned_ordinal is not None:
        _fenced(con, job, holder, "held", "hold",
               f"ordinal {owned_ordinal}: outcome={owned_outcome} — "
               "mopsus's own retry policy (ADR-0016/0021/plan 0029) "
               "owns this stop, not this worker")
        return

    with _mopsus_mod()._review_slot() as free:
        if not free:
            _fenced(con, job, holder, "held", "hold",
                   "mopsus's review-retry slot is held — waiting for it")
            return

        # Re-read NOW, inside the slot, rather than trusting `pending` above:
        # that was read BEFORE this worker had the slot, so a caller that
        # ALSO takes it before posting (mopsus's own `_dispatch_session_retry`,
        # its `kind="session"` retry) could have posted in the gap between
        # that read and this worker's own turn at the lock. This is what makes
        # "decide to spend" and "spend" atomic with respect to that one
        # caller — see the module docstring's "Automatic dispatch" section for
        # the callers this does NOT cover.
        reviews = _orch_mod().reviews_at(repo, pr, cur_sha, forge, fleetforge)
        if reviews is None:
            _fenced(con, job, holder, "held", "hold",
                   "existing reviews at this head are unreadable from the forge")
            return
        pending = _pending_ordinals(reviews, required)
        if not pending:
            _fenced(con, job, holder, "done", "done",
                   "every configured plan-reviewer seat's own review already "
                   f"stands at this head, seen after taking the retry slot "
                   f"({_seat_outcomes_reason(reviews, required)})")
            return
        owned_ordinal, owned_outcome = _owned_pending_ordinal(con, repo, pr, cur_sha, pending)
        if owned_ordinal is not None:
            _fenced(con, job, holder, "held", "hold",
                   f"ordinal {owned_ordinal}: outcome={owned_outcome} — "
                   "mopsus's own retry policy (ADR-0016/0021/plan 0029) "
                   "owns this stop, not this worker")
            return

        # A call into operator/talos: `dispatch-review.py <repo> <pr> --post
        # --no-followups`, via `orchestrator.dispatch_review()` (same argv, same
        # call `bin/orchestrator`'s own first-attempt review loop makes). Read
        # at `~/agent`, commit 2db65f5 (the same pin as the classifier port
        # above), to confirm what this call site relies on:
        #  - the argv is accepted for a PR the orchestrator holds no lease on:
        #    `main()` never checks one (grepped: no `lease`/`orchestrator`
        #    reference gates it) — only an open, non-merged PR is required.
        #  - a quota/no-verdict/budget stop writes its `review_attempt` row
        #    BEFORE the process exits non-zero (dispatch-review.py:5352-5367 for
        #    the single-ordinal path, :4560-4601 for the two-ordinal one) — the
        #    read `_owned_pending_ordinal` does above and after this call relies
        #    on that ordering, not a race with the exit code.
        #  - this argv passes no `--require-ci`, so talos's REQUIRED_CI gate
        #    (dispatch-review.py:4894-4899) never refuses here — it only WARNs
        #    — matching this worker's own `ci_verdict`, which was simplified on
        #    exactly that evidence (see its module comment above).
        #  - `standing_ordinals()` (dispatch-review.py:4391-4443, used at
        #    5121-5162) skips any ordinal already approved at this head, so a
        #    round that completes a PARTIAL prior one spends on the missing
        #    seat only — proven here only against the fake dispatcher in
        #    `tests/test_plan_review_queue.py`'s `post_accounts` mode, never
        #    against the real one, which this citation stands in for.
        ok, detail = _orch_mod().dispatch_review(repo, pr, cur_sha, None, timeout=DISPATCH_TIMEOUT)

        if not ok:
            owned_ordinal, owned_outcome = _owned_pending_ordinal(con, repo, pr, cur_sha, pending)
            if owned_ordinal is not None:
                _fenced(con, job, holder, "held", "hold",
                       f"ordinal {owned_ordinal}: outcome={owned_outcome} — "
                       "mopsus's own retry policy owns this stop, not "
                       "this worker", bump_attempt=True)
                return
            attempts = job["attempts"] + 1
            state = "failed" if attempts >= MAX_DISPATCH_ATTEMPTS else "held"
            _fenced(con, job, holder, state, "fail" if state == "failed" else "hold",
                   detail or "dispatch failed with no detail", bump_attempt=True)
            return

        reviews = _orch_mod().reviews_at(repo, pr, cur_sha, forge, fleetforge)
        pending = _pending_ordinals(reviews or [], required)
        if not pending:
            _fenced(con, job, holder, "done", "done",
                   "review posted and verified at this head "
                   f"({_seat_outcomes_reason(reviews or [], required)})")
            return
        owned_ordinal, owned_outcome = _owned_pending_ordinal(con, repo, pr, cur_sha, pending)
        if owned_ordinal is not None:
            _fenced(con, job, holder, "held", "hold",
                   f"ordinal {owned_ordinal}: outcome={owned_outcome} — "
                   "mopsus's own retry policy owns this stop, not this "
                   "worker", bump_attempt=True)
            return
        attempts = job["attempts"] + 1
        state = "failed" if attempts >= MAX_DISPATCH_ATTEMPTS else "held"
        pending_str = ", ".join(str(o) for o in pending)
        _fenced(con, job, holder, state, "fail" if state == "failed" else "hold",
               f"the dispatcher exited 0 but ordinal(s) {pending_str} still "
               "have no review at this head — exit is not a verdict",
               bump_attempt=True)


def _claim_jobs(con, holder, limit, claim_ttl):
    """Claim up to `limit` ready jobs — `queued`/`held`, or `claimed` past
    `claim_ttl` (an abandoned claim recovered the same way) — least-recently-
    touched first, one atomic `_transition` per job so a racing worker's loss
    is silent (D3). Returns the full row for each job actually won.

    Ordered by `updated_at`, NOT `created_at`: every `_transition` (a hold
    included) bumps `updated_at` to now, so a job held repeatedly — CI stuck
    red, a slow quota drain, anything that keeps re-qualifying as `held` sweep
    after sweep — sinks behind every job this worker has not touched yet,
    instead of keeping its spot at the front of the line forever because it
    happened to be filed first. `created_at` breaks ties so two untouched jobs
    still claim oldest-first, exactly as `created_at` ordering did on its own
    before any of them had been held once."""
    now = time.time()
    cutoff = now - claim_ttl
    candidates = con.execute(
        "SELECT id FROM plan_review_job WHERE state IN ('queued','held') "
        "OR (state='claimed' AND claimed_at < ?) ORDER BY updated_at, created_at LIMIT ?",
        (cutoff, max(limit * 4, limit))).fetchall()
    won = []
    for (job_id,) in candidates:
        if len(won) >= limit:
            break
        if _transition(con, job_id, "claimed", actor=holder, action="claim",
                       claim=True, holder=holder,
                       expect_states=OPEN_STATES, claim_cutoff=cutoff):
            row = con.execute(
                "SELECT id, repo, pr, head_sha, state, reason, attempts, "
                "claimed_by, claimed_at FROM plan_review_job WHERE id=?",
                (job_id,)).fetchone()
            won.append({"id": row[0], "repo": row[1], "pr": row[2], "head_sha": row[3],
                        "state": row[4], "reason": row[5], "attempts": row[6],
                        "claimed_by": row[7], "claimed_at": row[8]})
    return won


_FORGE = None


def _forge():
    """A `fleetforge.Forge`, built the same way `orch.reviews_at`'s own
    default (`forge=None`) builds one: `orch._forge()`, the orchestrator's
    authoring token (`token_for("authoring")`) and timeout. Reused rather than
    restated so this worker's forge reads can never disagree with the
    dispatcher's about the url, the token role or the timeout."""
    global _FORGE
    if _FORGE is None:
        try:
            _mod, _FORGE = _orch_mod()._forge()
        except _orch_mod().Stop as e:
            sys.exit(f"fleet-plan-review: {e}")
    return _FORGE


def cmd_enqueue(args):
    con = _connect(args.db)
    forge = _forge()
    st, pr_body = forge.get_pull(args.repo, args.pr)
    if st != 200 or not isinstance(pr_body, dict):
        sys.exit(f"fleet-plan-review: could not read {args.repo}#{args.pr} "
                 f"from the forge (status {st})")
    head_sha = ((pr_body.get("head") or {}).get("sha")) or None
    if not head_sha:
        sys.exit(f"fleet-plan-review: {args.repo}#{args.pr} has no readable head sha")
    job_id, created = _enqueue(con, args.repo, args.pr, head_sha)
    print(f"{'enqueued' if created else 'already tracked'} {args.repo}#{args.pr} "
          f"@ {head_sha[:8]} ({job_id})")
    return 0


def _row_dict(row, cols):
    return dict(zip(cols, row))


def cmd_status(args):
    con = _connect(args.db)
    cols = ("id", "repo", "pr", "head_sha", "state", "reason", "attempts",
            "claimed_by", "claimed_at", "created_at", "updated_at")
    sql = f"SELECT {', '.join(cols)} FROM plan_review_job"
    where, params = [], []
    if args.repo:
        where.append("repo=?")
        params.append(args.repo)
    if args.pr:
        where.append("pr=?")
        params.append(args.pr)
    if where:
        sql += " WHERE " + " AND ".join(where)
    sql += " ORDER BY created_at"
    rows = [_row_dict(r, cols) for r in con.execute(sql, params).fetchall()]
    if args.json:
        print(json.dumps(rows))
        return 0
    if not rows:
        print("no plan_review_job rows")
        return 0
    for r in rows:
        print(f"{r['repo']}#{r['pr']:<6} {r['head_sha'][:8]:9} {r['state']:11} "
              f"attempts={r['attempts']:<2} {r['reason'] or ''}")
    return 0


def cmd_worker(args):
    con = _connect(args.db)
    # Resolved (or refused, via `_forge`'s own `sys.exit` on a `Stop`) BEFORE
    # claiming anything: a token failure here must not strand jobs this
    # process already claimed, unworked, for the full `--claim-ttl` — nothing
    # claimed below is reachable by any OTHER worker until then either.
    _forge()
    holder = os.environ.get("EUNOMIA_SESSION") or f"pid:{os.getpid()}"
    jobs = _claim_jobs(con, holder, args.limit, args.claim_ttl)
    done = 0
    for job in jobs:
        try:
            _dispatch_one(con, job, holder)
            done += 1
        except Exception as e:
            # One job's exception must not strand the rest of this sweep's
            # claims for the full `--claim-ttl`, nor abort the batch with no
            # trace. Release this job back to `held`, fenced to `holder` so a
            # job already reclaimed by someone else before the exception is
            # left alone, and move on to the next claimed job.
            _fenced(con, job, holder, "held", "hold",
                   f"the worker raised {e.__class__.__name__}: {e}")
    print(f"fleet-plan-review: worker claimed {len(jobs)} job(s), "
          f"{done} completed without raising")
    return 0


def main(argv=None):
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="fleet-plan-review", description=__doc__.split("\n\n")[0])
    sub = ap.add_subparsers(dest="cmd", required=True)

    p = sub.add_parser("enqueue")
    p.add_argument("repo")
    p.add_argument("pr", type=int)
    p.add_argument("--db", default=None)

    p = sub.add_parser("status")
    p.add_argument("--repo", default=None)
    p.add_argument("--pr", type=int, default=None)
    p.add_argument("--json", action="store_true")
    p.add_argument("--db", default=None)

    p = sub.add_parser("worker")
    p.add_argument("--limit", type=int, default=5)
    p.add_argument("--claim-ttl", type=float, default=DEFAULT_CLAIM_TTL)
    p.add_argument("--db", default=None)

    args = ap.parse_args(argv)
    if args.db:
        args.db = os.path.expanduser(args.db)
    return {"enqueue": cmd_enqueue, "status": cmd_status, "worker": cmd_worker}[args.cmd](args)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
